Sure, here's an example of how you can modify the script to include the URL parameters you mentioned:


window.location.href = "https://example.com/?name=John&email=john@gmail.com&phone=1234567";

If you want to pass additional parameters to the script, you can simply add them on the existing URL using the ampersand (&) symbol, like this:


window.location.href = "https://example.com/?name=John&email=john@gmail.com&phone=1234567&param2=value2&param3=value3";

Make sure that the URL parameters you are passing are valid and do not exceed the maximum length supported by the URL encoding scheme used by the browser.  Please note that due to security concerns browsers may limit the number and length of URL parameters that can be passed as part of the "location" header.  This is not always a hard limit though
